"Alice died in 1927. In 1946, at eighty-seven, he married again, and he and his wife adopted two children. He died of pneumonia in New York in 1952, aged ninety-two. A Canadian historian who deplored his influence on schools wrote that he had been to his age what Aristotle was to the later Middle Ages: not a philosopher, but the philosopher. His prose is plain and often heavy, and he has been praised more often than read.": "9e99a02b843c",
"Back in New York, Dewey wrote steadily for the next thirty years, in books for scholars and in magazine articles for everyone else. He helped to found associations for university teachers and for civil liberties. In 1937, at the age of seventy-eight, he traveled to Mexico to chair a commission of inquiry into Stalins charges against Leon Trotsky. He disagreed with Trotskys politics, and the commission found the charges false. He published Art as Experience at seventy-five and a major work on logic at seventy-nine.": "2de9cb4629a1",
"Critics have not been wholly satisfied. If every warranted belief may be revised by later inquiry, it seems we never arrive at anything simply true, and we are owed some account of what inquiry is getting closer to. Dewey was content to say that it gets us out of the difficulty we were in, and into the next one.": "53a6e9c19ade",
"Dewey made much of this criticism himself. In Experience and Education (1938) he rebuked his own followers. He had never said, he wrote, that any activity a child enjoys is educational, or that teachers should stand back. The teachers task is harder in the new school, since she must know the subject thoroughly and also see how to lead a childs present interest toward it. Experience that goes nowhere is not education. The reply is fair to Dewey and leaves the practical problem standing. His method asks more of teachers than most systems are able to train or pay for.": "81b54277579c",
"Dewey never studied Buddhism closely, and yet a Buddhist reader finds much that is familiar. Both he and the Buddha distrust teaching that is accepted on authority and never tried. The Buddha told the Kālāmas to accept a teaching when you know for yourselves what it leads to, and he described his path as a training, something done. Both think of a person as a bundle of habits in motion with no fixed inner core. For Dewey character is the interpenetration of habits, formed by what we repeatedly do and open to being formed again. And both hold that the ordinary moment, fully attended to, is where the real work is. What Dewey calls an experience, complete and unified, is not far from what a meditator means by being present.": "9fbe74963b29",
"Education follows directly. If thinking begins with a real difficulty, a school that hands children answers to questions they have not asked will teach them to memorize and to obey. Children learn when they have something to do that matters to them and that cannot be done without thought. Dewey defined education as that reconstruction or reorganization of experience which adds to the meaning of experience, and which increases ability to direct the course of subsequent experience. Democracy is the same process on a larger scale: A democracy is more than a form of government; it is primarily a mode of associated living, of conjoint communicated experience. Art, finally, is experience at its most complete, when doing and undergoing are so joined that nothing is wasted.": "1f39a9b490c7",
"In 1919 Dewey took a sabbatical in Japan. Former students of his, among them Hu Shih, invited him to cross to China for a short visit. He and Alice landed in Shanghai on April 30. Four days later students in Peking took to the streets against the terms of the Versailles treaty, and the May Fourth Movement began. Dewey was fascinated. He stayed for more than two years, leaving in July 1921, and lectured in many provinces with Hu Shih and others interpreting. His lectures on education, democracy, and scientific method were printed in newspapers and sold as books.": "9971a6f7702c",
"In Chicago he did something few philosophers have done. In 1896 he and his wife Alice opened an elementary school in order to test a theory. The children cooked, built, sewed, and gardened, and arithmetic, chemistry, and history were taught as the problems arose from the work. Dewey called it the Laboratory School. He was also a trustee of Hull House, Jane Addamss settlement among the citys immigrants, and learned there what democracy looked like from below. In 1904, after a dispute with the university over the running of the school, he resigned and went to Columbia in New York, where he stayed for the rest of his career.": "bd813915fda4",
"John Dewey was born in Burlington, Vermont, in 1859, the son of a shopkeeper. He was named for a brother who had died in an accident nine months before. After college he taught high school for a few years, found that he was better at thinking about teaching than at keeping order, and went to Johns Hopkins for a doctorate in philosophy. He taught at Michigan and in 1894 moved to the new University of Chicago as head of a department that combined philosophy, psychology, and education.": "7e6110a878b9",
"No Western philosopher had been heard so widely in China before, and few have been since. His students carried his ideas into Chinese schools, and one of them, Tao Xingzhi, turned two of his slogans around. Dewey had said that education is life and the school a small society. Tao, working among the rural poor, said that life is education and society is the school. After 1949 Deweys name was attacked on the mainland together with Hu Shihs. His influence on education in Taiwan continued through Hu Shih and his colleagues.": "e28fcc829866",
"Older philosophies pictured the mind as a spectator that looks at the world from outside and tries to form a correct copy of it. Dewey, who had read Darwin, started from a living creature in an environment. Such a creature is always doing something. When its habits work, it does not think. When they fail and it does not know how to go on, it has a problem, and thinking is what it does to solve it: it forms a guess, acts on the guess, and sees what happens. Dewey called this inquiry, and held that science, morals, and politics are all forms of it. An idea is a plan of action, and it is tested by what follows when we act on it.": "446f3dc329e7",
"Pragmatism is often summed up as the view that a belief is true if it is useful to believe it. Bertrand Russell attacked it in that form: it would be useful to a tyrant that his subjects believe him wise, and that does not make it true. William James sometimes wrote in a way that invited the charge. Dewey took care to avoid it. He preferred to drop the word truth and speak of warranted assertibility: a claim is warranted when careful inquiry has tested it against the facts and it has held up. Usefulness to our wishes has nothing to do with it. What works, in his sense, is what solves the problem that started the inquiry, and the facts decide that.": "89160577c36d",
"The complaint against Deweys schools is as old as the schools. If lessons must grow out of what interests the child, the child may never be interested in the multiplication table, grammar, or the dates of anything. Projects take a great deal of time and cover little. Children from homes full of books will pick up the missing knowledge elsewhere, and children from poor homes will not, so that a method meant to be democratic widens the gap. Critics such as E. D. Hirsch have argued that shared knowledge has to be taught directly and in order, and that progressive methods gave American schools a generation of confident students who knew very little.": "6de6a2fd9b82",
"The difference is in where the process leads. Dewey refused to name a final goal. Growth, he said, has no end beyond more growth, and a school or a society is to be judged by whether it makes further growth possible. The Buddha did name a goal, the ending of craving and of suffering, and he measured every practice by whether it leads there. A Buddhist would say that Dewey describes very well how habits are remade and gives no reason to remake them in one direction and not another. Dewey would reply that fixed ends are the mark of the philosophies he was trying to leave behind. For a teacher the two can be used together. Dewey gives a method for how people learn, and the Dharma gives an account of what is worth learning (Dharma and Western Philosophy D18, D25).": "fd3adf6a2626",
